In article <2187@uwovax.uwo.ca> 13001_6035@uwovax.uwo.ca writes:
>I just got NetHack 2.3 from Grape.  I have noticed a large
>number of things I dislike about it.  One or two may be
>bugs.  Perhaps some of them are just misunderstandings.
>
>
>1. NETHACK.EXE is BIG!  V2.2 was about 350K, while this is
>   about 440K, making it unplayable without a HD.  I have
>   a HD, but not all my friends do.

This is because I compiled the game with ALL the optional compilation
(STOOGES, WIZARD, SAC, DGK, etc.) defined.  I could have made it smaller by
omitting them, but this would have resulted in numerous complaints like, "I
thought that 2.3e was supposed to have the 3 stooges in it but I can't find
them."  (Well, maybe not many would complain about THAT feature being omitted.
:-)

>2. When I enter a darkened room, I notice strange '
>   characters appearing and disappearing around the room.
>   The / (cursor select) command seems to have no effect
>   on these.

These are a bug which I considered too minor to delay posting the game as so
many were anxiously asking (begging, kicking, screaming :-) for it.  They
usually result from something (like your dog) moving around in the dark.  They
may also appear when you are in a corridor or near a wall, in which case they
indicate a secret door/passage you really shouldn't know about.  Searching
will make the ' character disappear.  These also trail monsters you see with
telepathy, but this is a feature, not a bug, as far as I am aware.

>3. The lighting in a darkened room is not uniform.  I have
>   seen light patterns like:    ...
>                                .@
>                                 ..  which are quite annoying.

I haven't noticed this before, but it may be related to the ' bug.

>4. I have had more hitpoints than my max, esp. after
>   polymorphing back into a human (e.g.  HP 36/35).

Also something I didn't notice during testing.  Is it permanent, or do your
HP's go back to normal when you get hit?

>5. On an IBM XT it appears slower than 2.2.

This may be the case, due to the larger size.  Speed can be greatly increased
by using a ramdisk, though.

>6. Your dog's polymorphing seems permanent, although yours
>   isn't.  (Weren't the stooges surprised when they hit
>   my dog polymorphed into a dragon:  Moe hits Nappie.
>   Nappie hits Moe.  Moe is dead.)

I also noticed this, and as far as I know, this is not a bug.  If it is,
someone please correct me.

>7. The stooges are stupid!  (Attacking my pet dragon!)

Agreed!

>8. The dog appears to have an infinite appetite.  (this
>   was also true in 2.2)

True, in that he (she? it?) will eat forever without choking to death, but
there is a set minimum which your dog needs before it starves to death.  If
it's eating all your food, why not ditch the poor mutt the first chance you
get?

>9. The kitchen sinks appear to have no use.  / (cursor
>   movement) misidentifies them as corridors.

The misidentification is due to the fact that the '#' symbol is a corridor
symbol on machines that don't have fancy PC graphics characters at their
disposal and should probably be corrected in 3.0.  They DO have a use.  Hint:
If you've got two identical, unidentified rings (e.g. two blackened rings),
drop one in a sink and watch what happens.

>10.Even when you polymorph to something that forces you
>   to drop your weapon, you can still pick it up, but
>   not wield it.

Don't see this as a problem.  If you are non-humanoid, you can still carry
stuff.  You just aren't able to weild/wear the same stuff as you could as a
human.

>11.When you are invisible, you should know where you are.
>   After all, you do see the walls, so you should be able
>   to orient yourself.

I suppose that this could be argued, but it may be equally arguable to say
that invisibility would be very disorienting.  In any case, just drop
something or use the '/' command and answer yes to the "...by cursor?" prompt
if you need help.

>12.The RUMOURS file got smaller.

Is this true?  I used the file included with the 2.3 source distribution from
uunet.uu.net.  Anyone know if 2.2+ had an enhanced file not a part of the
standard NetHack?

